partialText:
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Why an "untagged" lifecycle rule can remove parts of a tagged image built for several platforms in Amazon ECR and how to check your policy by hand before it does.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Lifecycle policies are how most of us keep an ECR bill sane. A rule like "expire untagged images older than 14 days" sounds harmless. For years it was.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then we started building for &lt;code&gt;linux/amd64&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;linux/arm64&lt;/code&gt; with Buildx. Buildx also adds provenance attestations by default. Now that same rule can remove pieces of an image you still deploy. You find out when a pull fails. Usually during a scale out or a rollback, which is the worst time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is why it happens and how I check a policy before I trust it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  One tag, many rows
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A push for several platforms does not create one image in ECR. It creates several manifests:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;an &lt;strong&gt;image index&lt;/strong&gt; (also called a manifest list). This is the only row with your tag.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;one &lt;strong&gt;image manifest per platform&lt;/strong&gt;, like &lt;code&gt;linux/amd64&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;linux/arm64&lt;/code&gt;. These hold the real layers.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;often an &lt;strong&gt;attestation manifest&lt;/strong&gt; per platform for provenance or an SBOM. In the index these show up with platform &lt;code&gt;unknown/unknown&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;if you sign images you may also get &lt;strong&gt;signatures&lt;/strong&gt; and other OCI referrers.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In the console and in &lt;code&gt;describe-images&lt;/code&gt; every one of these is its own row. Only the index has a tag. So the platform images and attestations look like random untagged images.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Lifecycle rules count rows. A rule with &lt;code&gt;"tagStatus": "untagged"&lt;/code&gt; selects every row with no tag. That includes the platform images your tagged index points to. Once they expire the tag still exists. Pulling it for that platform does not work anymore.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is not theory. In &lt;a href="https://github.com/aws/containers-roadmap/issues/1596" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;containers-roadmap #1596&lt;/a&gt; one user had a count rule remove two versions overnight and another reported a production outage in March 2026 after BuildKit started adding provenance rows to every build.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Look at what one tag really contains
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The quickest view is Buildx itself:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;docker buildx imagetools inspect \
  111122223333.dkr.ecr.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/my-repo:v1.4.0
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;You get the index digest and the digest of each platform image and attestation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Without Docker you can read the index straight from ECR:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;aws ecr batch-get-image --repository-name my-repo \
  --image-ids &lt;span class="nv"&gt;imageTag&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;v1.4.0 \
  --accepted-media-types application/vnd.oci.image.index.v1+json \
      application/vnd.docker.distribution.manifest.list.v2+json \
  --query &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'images[0].imageManifest'&lt;/span&gt; --output text \
&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; jq &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'.manifests[] | {digest, platform, type: .annotations["vnd.docker.reference.type"]}'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Rows where &lt;code&gt;type&lt;/code&gt; is &lt;code&gt;attestation-manifest&lt;/code&gt; are attestations. The rest are your platform images.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Now list the untagged rows with their media types:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;aws ecr describe-images --repository-name my-repo \
  --query &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'imageDetails[?imageTags==null].[imageDigest,imageManifestMediaType,artifactMediaType,imagePushedAt]'&lt;/span&gt; \
  --output table
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Compare the digests. Every untagged digest that appears inside a tagged index is a row you must keep. Do this for every tag you still care about.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  ECR's lifecycle preview and where it stops
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ECR has a built in preview:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;aws ecr start-lifecycle-policy-preview --repository-name my-repo \
  --lifecycle-policy-text file://policy.json

aws ecr get-lifecycle-policy-preview --repository-name my-repo
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;It runs async. You poll until the status is &lt;code&gt;COMPLETE&lt;/code&gt; and then get the digests and tags it would expire. It is useful. I run it. But keep its limits in mind:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It tells you what would go. It does not tell you what depends on it. Matching expiring digests against indexes is your job.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It knows nothing about ECS or Lambda. An image a service still runs looks the same as one nobody uses.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It shows today. An age rule that hits your running release next Tuesday does not show up.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It is one repository at a time and one preview per repository at a time. Many repos across regions and accounts means a script.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It needs &lt;code&gt;ecr:StartLifecyclePolicyPreview&lt;/code&gt;. The preview is stored on the AWS side. If you want a role that can only read, leave it out and simulate the rules yourself.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you simulate, follow ECR's rules. Rules run in &lt;code&gt;rulePriority&lt;/code&gt; order. A row is claimed by the first rule whose tag selection fits it and lower priority rules never touch it. &lt;code&gt;tagged&lt;/code&gt; rules need &lt;code&gt;tagPrefixList&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;tagPatternList&lt;/code&gt; and a row must match every entry. &lt;code&gt;imageCountMoreThan&lt;/code&gt; keeps the newest N rows the rule claimed, by push date. &lt;code&gt;sinceImagePushed&lt;/code&gt; expires rows older than N days.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Check what ECS and Lambda run
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before you apply anything, collect the images in use. For ECS services, read the task definitions:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;aws ecs describe-services --cluster prod --services api worker \
  --query &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'services[].taskDefinition'&lt;/span&gt; --output text \
&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; xargs -n1 aws ecs describe-task-definition --query &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'taskDefinition.containerDefinitions[].image'&lt;/span&gt; --output text --task-definition
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Running tasks report the exact digest they pulled:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;aws ecs describe-tasks --cluster prod \
  --tasks &lt;span class="nv"&gt;$(&lt;/span&gt;aws ecs list-tasks --cluster prod --desired-status RUNNING --query &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'taskArns'&lt;/span&gt; --output text&lt;span class="o"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; \
  --query &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'tasks[].containers[].[image,imageDigest]'&lt;/span&gt; --output text
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;For Lambda functions packaged as container images:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;aws lambda get-function --function-name my-fn \
  --query &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Code.[ImageUri,ResolvedImageUri]'&lt;/span&gt; --output text
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Turn every tag into a digest with &lt;code&gt;describe-images&lt;/code&gt;. Then make sure none of those digests are in the preview. Also check the platform images under them. A running &lt;code&gt;v1.2.0&lt;/code&gt; is broken just as badly if its arm64 image goes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A safer policy
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is no untagged rule that is safe in every repo. The idea that works for me: &lt;strong&gt;untagged rows must outlive the tagged images that point to them.&lt;/strong&gt; That only holds if every tagged image in the repo has an age limit shorter than the untagged rule.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So I keep short lived builds and releases apart. In a repo for CI builds:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"rules"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"rulePriority"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"description"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Branch and dev builds go after 30 days"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"selection"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"tagStatus"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"tagged"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"tagPatternList"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"dev-*"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"countType"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"sinceImagePushed"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"countUnit"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"days"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"countNumber"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;30&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"action"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"type"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"expire"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"rulePriority"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"description"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Untagged rows go only after their parents can be gone"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"selection"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"tagStatus"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"untagged"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"countType"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"sinceImagePushed"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"countUnit"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"days"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"countNumber"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;45&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"action"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"type"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"expire"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;When a &lt;code&gt;dev-*&lt;/code&gt; index expires at 30 days its children become orphans. The untagged rule cleans them up later. The 14 day gap is the margin.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This only works if every tag in that repo matches &lt;code&gt;dev-*&lt;/code&gt;. Any other tag has no age limit here, so its children get hit at 45 days. That is why releases live in their own repo. There I keep tagged releases with a count rule and either no untagged rule at all or one with an age longer than my oldest kept release. I check that age again whenever I raise the count.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two more things help. If you do not need provenance attestations, &lt;code&gt;docker buildx build --provenance=false&lt;/code&gt; stops creating them. And never use &lt;code&gt;tagStatus: any&lt;/code&gt; with a count rule on a repo that holds images for several platforms. It counts platform images and attestations as images.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Run the preview again after every policy change. In every region and every account.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Disclosure: I built a tool for this
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Doing all of the above for a few dozen repos got old, so I built &lt;a href="https://ecrinspect.puzaricloud.in" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ECR Image Inspector and Lifecycle Policy Dry-Run&lt;/a&gt;. It connects with a CloudFormation role that can only read ECR, ECS and Lambda metadata. It groups each repo into index, platform images and attestations, signatures and SBOMs. It simulates your real policy with the rules described above without calling the preview API. Then it shows what would expire today and in the next 7 days. It warns when a row is used by an ECS service, a running task or a Lambda function. It also warns when a row belongs to a tagged image you keep. It suggests a safer policy and checks it the same way. A daily scan alerts on Slack or email. There is a free trial. I built it, so take my take with that in mind. The CLI steps above are all you strictly need.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;TL;DR:&lt;/strong&gt; There is no documented Google Autocomplete API. The endpoint everyone copies from Stack Overflow, &lt;code&gt;suggestqueries.google.com/complete/search&lt;/code&gt;, is one Google called "unpublished" and "unsupported" when it restricted outside use in 2015. It still answers. I ran one seed keyword across Google, YouTube, Bing and Google Shopping: 299 suggestions in 6 seconds, and the phrases that show up on every engine are the ones worth writing about. Code and output below.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  "Where is the documentation?"
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is the literal title of a &lt;a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/5102878/where-is-the-documentation-for-the-google-suggest-api" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Stack Overflow question from 24 February 2011&lt;/a&gt;. It had 93,109 views on 22 November 2026. The answer is still the same: there isn't any.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Google said it plainly on &lt;a href="https://developers.google.com/search/blog/2015/07/update-on-autocomplete-api" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;24 July 2015&lt;/a&gt;. The post calls it a non-official, non-published API and announced it "will be restricting unauthorized access to the unpublished autocomplete API" from 10 August 2015. Google's reason: completions are meant to sit next to search results, and outside that they add little.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So what do you actually get today?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Question&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Answer (checked 22 Nov 2026)&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Official, documented Google autocomplete API?&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;No&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Does &lt;code&gt;suggestqueries.google.com&lt;/code&gt; still return suggestions?&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Yes, from Apify servers, for Google, YouTube (&lt;code&gt;ds=yt&lt;/code&gt;) and Shopping (&lt;code&gt;ds=sh&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Rate limits, SLA, terms for this use?&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Not published. Google can change or block it any time&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Is one call enough for keyword research?&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;No. One call gives about 10 suggestions. You need A–Z, question and preposition expansions&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  One keyword, four engines
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I ran "standing desk" with the seed plus question expansions (how, what, why, is, are...), US English:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Engine&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Suggestions&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Google&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;151&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Bing&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;80&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;YouTube&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;52&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Google Shopping&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;16&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Total&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;299 (243 unique)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Run time 6 seconds, cost $0.075.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The interesting part is the overlap. These came up on &lt;strong&gt;all four&lt;/strong&gt; engines:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;standing desk chair&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;standing desk amazon&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;standing desk with drawers&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And these on three: legs, frame, topper, converter, with storage, "does standing desk help", "is standing desk healthy", "are standing desks worth it", "are standing desks good for you".&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A phrase that people type into Google, Bing and YouTube is real demand, not one engine's quirk. That list is a content brief.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The top questions, in Google's order: "does standing desk help", "is standing desk healthy", "are standing desks worth it", "are standing desks good for you", "what standing desk to buy". Hello, FAQ section.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The code
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I didn't call Google from my own machine. Hammering an unsupported endpoint from one IP gets you rate-limited fast. This runs on Apify instead (apify-client 3.x):&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;os&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;apify_client&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ApifyClient&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;client&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ApifyClient&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;os&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;environ&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"APIFY_TOKEN"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;actor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"kuezi/google-autocomplete-scraper"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;call&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;run_input&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"keywords"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"standing desk"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"engines"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"google"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"youtube"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"bing"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"google_shopping"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"expansions"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"seed"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"questions"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"language"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"en"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"country"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"us"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;rows&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;list&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;dataset&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;default_dataset_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;iterate_items&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;len&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;rows&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"suggestions"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;rows&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"engine"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"position"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"suggestion"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;summary&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;key_value_store&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;default_key_value_store_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get_record&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"SUMMARY"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;summary&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"value"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"keywords"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"crossEngine"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;5&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Real output (first lines):&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;299 suggestions
google 1 standing desk reviews
google 2 standing desk converter
google 3 standing desk with drawers
google 4 standing desk topper
google 5 standing desk with storage
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Each row has the engine, the expanded query, the suggestion, its &lt;code&gt;position&lt;/code&gt;, and for Google a &lt;code&gt;relevance&lt;/code&gt; score and suggestion type. The &lt;code&gt;SUMMARY&lt;/code&gt; record holds the cross-engine list and the top questions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://apify.com/kuezi/google-autocomplete-scraper" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Google Autocomplete Scraper on Apify&lt;/a&gt;. Disclosure: I built this. It charges $0.00025 per suggestion ($0.25 per 1,000), a bit less on paid Apify plans, and it also covers Yahoo, DuckDuckGo and Wikipedia.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Limits, honestly
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;It's unofficial.&lt;/strong&gt; Google never documented the endpoint and can change it. If it breaks, every tool built on it breaks, mine included.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Suggestions are not search volume.&lt;/strong&gt; They tell you what people type, not how many. Pair them with &lt;a href="https://apify.com/kuezi/google-trends-scraper" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Google Trends&lt;/a&gt; for direction, or a paid keyword tool for volume.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Results move.&lt;/strong&gt; Suggestions depend on country, language and date. Re-run before you publish a brief.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Shopping is thin.&lt;/strong&gt; 16 suggestions for a product keyword. Use it for buyer phrases, not coverage.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you know of an official replacement I missed, tell me in the comments and I'll update this.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;AI disclosure: I wrote this with AI assistance. Every quote, number and output above was checked or run on 22 November 2026.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Use CloudTrail to find the calls your AI coding agent made, spot the risky ones and turn what it really used into a smaller IAM role.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In February 2026 Alexey Grigorev of DataTalks.Club shared a hard story. An AI coding agent ran &lt;code&gt;terraform destroy&lt;/code&gt; and took out a production database along with its snapshots. He wrote it up openly in &lt;a href="https://rootly.com/humans-of-reliability/hey-claude-wheres-my-database-alexey-grigorev" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;this interview with Rootly&lt;/a&gt;. I respect him for that. Most of us have handed an agent our credentials and hoped for the best. I know I have.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Elastic now ships a &lt;a href="https://www.elastic.co/guide/en/security/8.19/potential-destructive-aws-cli-command-executed-by-genai-agent.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;detection rule&lt;/a&gt; for destructive AWS CLI commands run by a GenAI agent. That tells you this is not a rare case anymore.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So here is the question I kept asking myself. If an agent did something in my account last night, could I tell? Most of the time the answer was no. The agent runs with my SSO admin role. In CloudTrail its calls look exactly like mine.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is how I dig them out by hand.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The CloudTrail fields that matter
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every account has CloudTrail event history. It holds 90 days of management events per region and costs nothing. These fields do most of the work:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;userAgent&lt;/code&gt;: the AWS CLI and SDKs put their name and version here. Some agent tools and MCP servers add their own marker. Many do not. Treat it as a hint, never as proof.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;userIdentity.arn&lt;/code&gt;: for an assumed role this ends with the role session name. If agents start sessions with a name like &lt;code&gt;agent-ritom&lt;/code&gt; you can filter on it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;userIdentity.accessKeyId&lt;/code&gt;: keys starting with &lt;code&gt;ASIA&lt;/code&gt; are temporary and belong to one session. Keys starting with &lt;code&gt;AKIA&lt;/code&gt; are static user keys. An agent on an &lt;code&gt;AKIA&lt;/code&gt; key is a finding on its own.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;sourceIPAddress&lt;/code&gt;: your laptop, a CI runner or a cloud sandbox. Handy when the agent runs somewhere you do not.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;readOnly&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;errorCode&lt;/code&gt;: writes are what you care about first. &lt;code&gt;AccessDenied&lt;/code&gt; tells you what the agent tried and could not do. That is often the most useful part.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One gotcha up front. IAM and other global services log to us-east-1. Always check that region too.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Pull the calls with the CLI and jq
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start with the write calls for one person in one region:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;aws cloudtrail lookup-events --region us-east-1 \
  --lookup-attributes &lt;span class="nv"&gt;AttributeKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;Username,AttributeValue=jane@example.com \
  --start-time 2026-11-20T00:00:00Z \
  --query &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Events[].CloudTrailEvent'&lt;/span&gt; --output json \
&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; jq -r &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'.[] | fromjson | select(.readOnly==false)
    | [.eventTime, .eventSource, .eventName, .userIdentity.accessKeyId,
       .sourceIPAddress, .userAgent, (.errorCode // "-")] | @tsv'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Then group by access key. With temporary credentials each key is one session, so this gives you a rough list of sessions with their user agents:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;aws cloudtrail lookup-events --region us-east-1 \
  --start-time 2026-11-20T00:00:00Z \
  --query &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Events[].CloudTrailEvent'&lt;/span&gt; --output json \
&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; jq -r &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'[.[] | fromjson] | group_by(.userIdentity.accessKeyId)[]
    | {key: .[0].userIdentity.accessKeyId, who: .[0].userIdentity.arn,
       agents: ([.[].userAgent] | unique), calls: length,
       writes: ([.[] | select(.readOnly==false)] | length)}'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;lookup-events&lt;/code&gt; takes one lookup attribute per call and is rate limited. It is fine for one person and one day. It gets slow for a busy account.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Query a trail with Athena
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you have a trail writing to S3 you can create an Athena table from the CloudTrail console. Then you can search everything at once:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight sql"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;SELECT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;eventtime&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;eventsource&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;eventname&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
       &lt;span class="n"&gt;useridentity&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;arn&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;AS&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;principal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
       &lt;span class="n"&gt;useridentity&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;accesskeyid&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;AS&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
       &lt;span class="n"&gt;sourceipaddress&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useragent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;errorcode&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;FROM&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;cloudtrail_logs&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;WHERE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;readonly&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'false'&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;AND&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;eventtime&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'2026-11-20T00:00:00Z'&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;AND&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;regexp_like&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;lower&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;useragent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'claude|cursor|copilot|codex|gemini|kiro|mcp|langchain'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
       &lt;span class="k"&gt;OR&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;regexp_like&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;lower&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;useridentity&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;arn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'/(agent|claude|mcp)[^/]*$'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;ORDER&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;BY&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;eventtime&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;CloudTrail Lake has the same fields with camel case names. The same idea works there.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Which calls should scare you
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not every write is a problem. These are the ones I alert on:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;anything on &lt;code&gt;iam.amazonaws.com&lt;/code&gt; that is not a read&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;Delete*&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;Terminate*&lt;/code&gt; calls, like &lt;code&gt;DeleteDBInstance&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;DeleteDBSnapshot&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;TerminateInstances&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;AuthorizeSecurityGroupIngress&lt;/code&gt; with &lt;code&gt;0.0.0.0/0&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;::/0&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;DisableKey&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;ScheduleKeyDeletion&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;PutKeyPolicy&lt;/code&gt; on KMS&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;changes to S3 Block Public Access&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;StopLogging&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;DeleteTrail&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;UpdateTrail&lt;/code&gt; on CloudTrail&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;any Organizations write&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Five or more &lt;code&gt;AccessDenied&lt;/code&gt; errors in one session also deserve a look. That usually means the agent was trying different routes to get something done.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Build a smaller role from what the agent really used
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Once you know what the agent calls, give it its own role. IAM Access Analyzer can write the first draft of the policy from CloudTrail. It needs a trail and a service role that can read the trail bucket:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;aws accessanalyzer start-policy-generation \
  --policy-generation-details &lt;span class="nv"&gt;principalArn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;arn:aws:iam::111122223333:role/agent-dev \
  --cloud-trail-details &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'{
    "trails": [{"cloudTrailArn": "arn:aws:cloudtrail:us-east-1:111122223333:trail/main", "allRegions": true}],
    "accessRole": "arn:aws:iam::111122223333:role/AccessAnalyzerTrailRead",
    "startTime": "2026-10-23T00:00:00Z"
  }'&lt;/span&gt;

aws accessanalyzer get-generated-policy --job-id &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;job id from above&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Review what comes back. I keep only the reads for the agent role. If the agent truly needs to write, I do that step myself or give it a separate role that I assume on purpose.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Guardrails that stop the next one
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A role for reading only.&lt;/strong&gt; Set &lt;code&gt;MaxSessionDuration&lt;/code&gt; to 3600 so sessions end after an hour. Start sessions with a clear name:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;aws sts assume-role --role-arn arn:aws:iam::111122223333:role/agent-dev \
  --role-session-name agent-jane --source-identity jane
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A label in the user agent.&lt;/strong&gt; Set &lt;code&gt;AWS_SDK_UA_APP_ID=my-agent&lt;/code&gt; in the shell the agent uses. The AWS CLI v2 and the SDKs add it to the user agent so the calls stand out in CloudTrail.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A permissions boundary.&lt;/strong&gt; Attach one to every agent role. Then even if the agent finds an IAM write it can use, it cannot give itself more than the boundary allows.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;An SCP for the worst calls.&lt;/strong&gt; In AWS Organizations you can deny destructive calls for every role whose name starts with &lt;code&gt;agent-&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"Version"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"2012-10-17"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"Statement"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"Sid"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"NoDestructiveCallsFromAgents"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"Effect"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Deny"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"Action"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"rds:DeleteDBInstance"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"rds:DeleteDBCluster"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"rds:DeleteDBSnapshot"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"rds:DeleteDBClusterSnapshot"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"ec2:TerminateInstances"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"s3:DeleteBucket"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"cloudtrail:StopLogging"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"cloudtrail:DeleteTrail"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"kms:ScheduleKeyDeletion"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"iam:*"&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"Resource"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"*"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"Condition"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"ArnLike"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"aws:PrincipalArn"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"arn:aws:iam::*:role/agent-*"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Also turn on deletion protection for production databases and keep backups somewhere the agent role cannot touch. None of this helps if the agent still runs as your admin role. That is the first thing to change.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A few more gotchas
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Event history covers management events only. S3 object reads need data events on a trail.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Events land a few minutes after the call.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A user agent can be set to anything. Session names and roles are much more reliable.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Disclosure: I built a tool for this
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I got tired of running these queries by hand, so I built &lt;a href="https://agentaudit.puzaricloud.in" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AI Agent Activity Audit&lt;/a&gt;. You connect an account with a CloudFormation role that can only read CloudTrail event history and IAM role details. Every 10 minutes it picks out agent calls by user agent, session name, roles you mark as agent roles or your own patterns. It shows why each one matched. It groups them into sessions, alerts on Slack or email for the risky calls above, flags agents on admin roles or static keys and drafts a role for reading only from what the agent actually called. It needs no trail. There is a 14 day free trial. I am the person who built it, so weigh my opinion with that in mind. Everything above works fine without it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Originally published on &lt;a href="https://cluetrove.com/why-is-ai-generated-english-hard-to-read-study-inanimate-subjects/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ClueTrove&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;All nine frontier and near-frontier models in a new draft study use more inanimate subjects with animate verbs, and more low-frequency word sequences, than human writers do. “The report argues” is the pattern. The study measures English only and tests no human readers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The authors are Thomas Hikaru Clark, Matias Martin and Kyle Mahowald (arXiv, 21 October 2026, version 1). The arXiv page lists no journal reference, so treat it as not peer-reviewed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The short answer
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Language:&lt;/strong&gt; English only.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Text compared:&lt;/strong&gt; multi-turn chat, multi-turn agentic coding, and single-reply essays from nine frontier and near-frontier models.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Human baselines:&lt;/strong&gt; OpenSubtitles (chat), StackExchange (coding), PERSUADE 2.0 student essays (essays), and Wikipedia.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Main control:&lt;/strong&gt; a round-trip translation paraphrase (English to Japanese to English with NLLB-200), which keeps the meaning but changes the wording.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Result:&lt;/strong&gt; all nine models use more inanimate subjects with animate verbs and more low-frequency n-grams than human writing. The effect is smaller in single-prompt essays than in chat and coding.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Caveat:&lt;/strong&gt; the authors say they rely on proxies to infer the cause.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Which features did the study count?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The paper measures features linked to processing difficulty in human readers:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;rare or unattested n-grams and n-gram perplexity;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;dependency length;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;object relative clauses, with and without “that”;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;stacked noun compounds;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;denominal verbs (nouns used as verbs);&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;inanimate subjects with animate verbs, for example “the report argues”.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Compared with round-trip paraphrases, most frontier models overuse rare n-grams, denominal verbs, inanimate subjects and reduced object relatives. They underuse object relatives that keep the word “that”. The authors read this as a preference for dropping optional words.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Do all models do it equally?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No. Effects vary by model family. Llama and the Olmo base and instruct models change least. Olmo Think, a reasoning model, patterns with the frontier models, and the Olmo effects grow from base to instruct to reasoning.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The paper reports that Claude Opus 5.5 scores lower than Opus 5 on 19 of 21 difficulty-linked comparisons, with non-overlapping 95% intervals in eight. That is a result for those measures only, not a statement that one model writes better.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why might it happen? The paper’s small experiment
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The authors suspect pressure for information density during post-training. Their abstract says AI text is increasingly optimized for math, code and reasoning, which favors density over successful communication.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To test the idea at small scale, they fine-tuned Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct with GRPO under a length penalty on a sentence-generation task. Without KL regularization, the model came to prefer the shorter variant (dropping “that”) in over 98% of cases at every penalty value above zero. Some of that preference transferred to constructions it was not trained on. The paper notes it could not study models at real post-training scale, so this supports the idea without proving the cause.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What can writers and editors take from it?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The paper does not give a style guide. The suggestions below are our editing reading of its measures, not findings of the study:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;We suggest a person or thing that can act as the subject over an abstraction (“the team found” over “the analysis shows”).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;We suggest keeping the word “that” when removing it makes a clause harder to parse.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;We suggest replacing stacked nouns and nouns used as verbs with a plain phrase.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;We suggest breaking sentences where the verb sits far from its subject.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To check whether a model’s output suits your readers, test it on your own tasks as in &lt;a href="https://cluetrove.com/how-to-run-a-one-afternoon-ai-model-evaluation/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;how to run a one-afternoon AI model evaluation&lt;/a&gt;. Read comparison tables with &lt;a href="https://cluetrove.com/five-ways-ai-benchmark-tables-mislead/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;five ways AI benchmark tables mislead&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What are the limits of the study?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Only a subset of models was tested, and all analyses are in English, so nothing here applies to Arabic.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The chat and coding conversations use a simulated user (Claude Sonnet 4.6) and a limited number of seeds.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The measures are proxies for reading difficulty. This study did not test human readers.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What to do next
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Check the arXiv page for newer versions before you cite the study.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Test a model on your own tasks and readers before you judge its writing.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Questions and answers about the study
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Does this mean AI writing is worse?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not by itself. The paper reports more of certain features known to slow human processing, not a quality score.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Does it apply to Arabic?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No. The authors analyzed English only.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Is the cause settled?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No. The authors propose information-density pressure in post-training and test it only in a small model.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Did the study test human readers?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No. The measures are proxies for reading difficulty.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Which model came out best?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The paper gives no overall winner. It reports Opus 5.5 below Opus 5 on 19 of 21 difficulty-linked comparisons, and Llama and the Olmo base and instruct models change least.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Terms in plain language
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;n-gram:&lt;/strong&gt; a sequence of consecutive words.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Perplexity:&lt;/strong&gt; how surprising a word sequence is to a language model.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Inanimate subject with an animate verb:&lt;/strong&gt; a thing doing a human action, as in “the report argues”.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Post-training:&lt;/strong&gt; the training stage after the base model, used to shape how it answers.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Preprint:&lt;/strong&gt; a paper shared before peer review.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Related reading
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://cluetrove.com/pilotdeck-2-editable-memory-routing-cost-study-limits/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;PilotDeck 2.0: Editable Memory, Routing, Cost Study Limits&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Sources
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29162" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;arXiv: Why is AI-generated language so hard to understand? (21 October 2026)&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2610.29162v1" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;arXiv full text, v1&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Facts read on 26 October 2026. We did not run any experiment.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Originally published at &lt;a href="https://unbreakkit.netlify.app/guides/supabase-42501-permission-denied-new-tables.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;unbreakkit.netlify.app&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The error
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You create a new table (in the SQL editor, a migration, or because Lovable, Bolt, Cursor or another AI tool made it), and your app's first query fails:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"code"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"42501"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"message"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"permission denied for table your_table"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"hint"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Grant the required privileges to the current role with: GRANT SELECT ON public.your_table TO anon;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Older tables in the same project still work. Only the new one is broken.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why it happens
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Supabase announced a change to a default. Previously, every new table in the &lt;code&gt;public&lt;/code&gt; schema was automatically granted &lt;code&gt;select&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;insert&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;update&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;delete&lt;/code&gt; for the &lt;code&gt;anon&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;authenticated&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;service_role&lt;/code&gt; roles, so the Data API (what &lt;code&gt;supabase-js&lt;/code&gt; calls) could reach it immediately.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Per Supabase's changelog, new projects were scheduled to stop getting those automatic grants from May 30, 2026, and &lt;strong&gt;the same change was scheduled to be applied to all existing projects on October 30, 2026.&lt;/strong&gt; As of November 22, 2026, official Data API docs still say existing projects auto-grant new &lt;code&gt;public&lt;/code&gt; tables, and Supabase has not confirmed that the October 30 rollout happened. If the change has reached your project, a new table in &lt;code&gt;public&lt;/code&gt; is invisible to the Data API until you run an explicit &lt;code&gt;GRANT&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In plain English, there are now two separate locks:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;GRANT&lt;/strong&gt; decides whether a role (&lt;code&gt;anon&lt;/code&gt; = logged-out visitors, &lt;code&gt;authenticated&lt;/code&gt; = signed-in users) can touch the table at all. Missing grant = &lt;code&gt;42501 permission denied for table&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;RLS policies&lt;/strong&gt; decide &lt;em&gt;which rows&lt;/em&gt; that role can see or change. Missing policy = empty results, or &lt;code&gt;new row violates row-level security policy&lt;/code&gt; (which is also code &lt;code&gt;42501&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You need both. What did &lt;strong&gt;not&lt;/strong&gt; change: existing tables keep their grants, RLS works the same as before, and apps that only use a direct Postgres connection (psql, an ORM, a server with a connection string) aren't affected.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Fixes
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  1. Check which grants the table has
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Run this in the Supabase SQL editor:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight sql"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;select&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;grantee&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;privilege_type&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;information_schema&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;role_table_grants&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;where&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;table_schema&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'public'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;and&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;table_name&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'your_table'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;If &lt;code&gt;anon&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;authenticated&lt;/code&gt; is missing from the list, that's your 42501. Also read the &lt;code&gt;hint&lt;/code&gt; in the error: it names the exact role and privilege that's missing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  2. Grant only what each role needs
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Make sure the roles can use the schema (normally already true on Supabase):&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight sql"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;grant&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;usage&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;on&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;schema&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;to&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;anon&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;authenticated&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Private data that only signed-in users touch (most app tables):&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight sql"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;grant&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;select&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;insert&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;update&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;delete&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;on&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;table&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;your_table&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;to&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;authenticated&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;grant&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;select&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;insert&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;update&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;delete&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;on&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;table&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;your_table&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;to&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;service_role&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Public read-only data (for example a product list visitors can browse):&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight sql"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;grant&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;select&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;on&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;table&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;products&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;to&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;anon&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;authenticated&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Don't grant &lt;code&gt;anon&lt;/code&gt; write access unless logged-out visitors truly need to write (for example a public contact form, and then only &lt;code&gt;insert&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  3. Turn RLS on and write policies scoped to auth.uid()
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A grant opens the door to the whole table, so RLS must decide which rows. For a table with a &lt;code&gt;user_id&lt;/code&gt; column:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight sql"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;alter&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;table&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;your_table&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;enable&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;row&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;level&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;security&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;create&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;policy&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Users read own rows"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;on&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;your_table&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;select&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;to&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;authenticated&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;using&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;select&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;auth&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;uid&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;user_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;create&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;policy&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Users insert own rows"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;on&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;your_table&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;insert&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;to&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;authenticated&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;with&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;check&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;select&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;auth&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;uid&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;user_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;create&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;policy&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Users update own rows"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;on&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;your_table&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;update&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;to&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;authenticated&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;using&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;select&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;auth&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;uid&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;user_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;with&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;check&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;select&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;auth&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;uid&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;user_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;create&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;policy&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Users delete own rows"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;on&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;your_table&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;delete&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;to&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;authenticated&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;using&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;select&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;auth&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;uid&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;user_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;For public read-only data:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight sql"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;alter&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;table&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;products&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;enable&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;row&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;level&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;security&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;create&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;policy&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Anyone can read products"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;on&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;products&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;select&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;to&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;anon&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;authenticated&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;using&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


  4. Add the GRANT to every future migration
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;From now on, every &lt;code&gt;create table&lt;/code&gt; your AI tool writes needs its &lt;code&gt;grant&lt;/code&gt; lines (and RLS policies) in the same migration. Otherwise each new table breaks the same way. Supabase notes this applies however the table was created: SQL editor, migrations, CLI, MCP or an AI coding tool.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  5. Don't "fix" it by disabling RLS or using the service role key in the browser
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AI agents often "fix" this error by running &lt;code&gt;alter table ... disable row level security&lt;/code&gt;, granting everything to &lt;code&gt;anon&lt;/code&gt;, or pasting the &lt;code&gt;service_role&lt;/code&gt; / secret key into frontend code. The error goes away, but your data becomes readable (and often writable) by anyone who has your public anon key, which every visitor's browser has. Disabling RLS also doesn't fix a missing grant: it's the wrong lock. Keep RLS on, grant the minimum, and write the policies you need.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Prompt to paste into Lovable, Bolt or Cursor
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;My Supabase query on the "your_table" table fails with error 42501 "permission denied for table". Supabase no longer grants Data API access to new public tables automatically, so this needs an explicit GRANT. First show me the current grants (information_schema.role_table_grants) and RLS policies for this table. Then write one SQL migration that: grants only the privileges the app needs to the authenticated role (and anon only for public read-only data), keeps RLS enabled, and adds select/insert/update/delete policies scoped to (select auth.uid()) = user_id. Do NOT disable RLS, do NOT grant write access to anon, and do NOT put the service_role key in client code. Also add the same grant pattern to any other tables you create from now on.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  FAQ
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Why do my old tables still work?&lt;/strong&gt; Existing tables keep the grants they already had. The change only affects tables created after it reached your project.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;I added an RLS policy but still get 42501. Why?&lt;/strong&gt; Policies don't help if the role has no GRANT on the table. Check the grants first (step 1). If the grant exists and inserts still fail with "new row violates row-level security policy", that's the policy side: see our &lt;a href="https://unbreakkit.netlify.app/guides/supabase-empty-data-no-error-rls.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;empty data / RLS guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Is granting to anon dangerous?&lt;/strong&gt; Only without RLS. A grant lets the role reach the table, and RLS limits the rows. Grant &lt;code&gt;anon&lt;/code&gt; only what logged-out visitors need, usually &lt;code&gt;select&lt;/code&gt; on public data.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Can I just turn the old behavior back on?&lt;/strong&gt; Supabase's docs still describe an "Automatically expose new tables" setting in the Data API settings, and the CLI &lt;code&gt;auto_expose_new_tables&lt;/code&gt; flag is still present â€” the planned October 30, 2026 removal was withdrawn in CLI v2.116.0. Explicit grants are the durable fix either way.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Does this affect my server that connects with a connection string?&lt;/strong&gt; No. Per Supabase, direct Postgres connections aren't affected; it's the Data API (&lt;code&gt;supabase-js&lt;/code&gt;, REST, GraphQL) that needs the grants.&lt;/p&gt;

