&lt;p&gt;When RAG answers stay stale after updating docs, debug the reindex as a versioned delivery pipeline: attach one revision ID to the page fetch, running queue job, index commit, retrieval result, and answer citation. If a property-management wiki assistant still quotes an old leasing-policy page, find the first stage whose recorded revision falls behind. Do not start by tuning prompts or rebuilding the entire corpus.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;TL;DR:&lt;/strong&gt; prove that the watcher observed the changed page, the queue accepted one durable job, workers committed the new chunks, and queries can see that commit. A newer source revision paired with an older retrieved revision localizes the fault quickly. Reindex only the affected document, make that operation idempotent, and keep the prior revision available until verification passes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why are RAG answers stale after updating docs?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A RAG answer depends on retrieved external memory, not just model behavior. The original RAG paper describes generation conditioned on retrieved passages; if retrieval returns an old passage, a fluent answer can faithfully repeat stale material. Freshness is a data-delivery property.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a property manager, the symptom might be an assistant citing yesterday's pet policy after the public policy page changed this morning. Several failures look identical from the chat box: the watcher missed the change, normalization erased the meaningful diff, a queue job was lost, an older job overwrote a newer one, the index commit was not visible, or a cache served an earlier retrieval result. The answer alone cannot distinguish them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I use the same reflex that applies to scheduled work: a trigger is not evidence of completion. A watcher log saying &lt;code&gt;changed=true&lt;/code&gt; proves observation, not indexing. An acknowledged queue message proves acceptance under the queue's contract, not that a searchable commit exists.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is why a blind full rebuild is a poor first response. It may restore freshness, but it destroys evidence and spends indexing capacity on unchanged leases, notices, and building pages. At portfolio scale, that cost becomes the operational constraint.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start there.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Carry one revision across every boundary
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Derive a stable source revision from the content that should affect retrieval. Normalize intentionally: remove volatile page furniture such as a generated timestamp, but retain policy text and headings. Hash the normalized bytes, then carry that hash through every event and stored record. The URL identifies the document; the revision identifies its state.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The worker below shows the important properties without depending on a particular queue or index. It rejects an obsolete delivery, skips an already committed revision, and swaps the document's chunks as one logical commit.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight go"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;package&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;indexing&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"context"&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"errors"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Job&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;struct&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nc"&gt;DocumentID&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Revision&lt;/span&gt;   &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Body&lt;/span&gt;       &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;byte&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Chunk&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;struct&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nc"&gt;DocumentID&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Revision&lt;/span&gt;   &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Position&lt;/span&gt;   &lt;span class="kt"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Text&lt;/span&gt;       &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Vector&lt;/span&gt;     &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;float32&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Index&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;interface&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nc"&gt;CurrentRevision&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ReplaceDocument&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Chunk&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;RevisionOrder&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;interface&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Older&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;candidate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;bool&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Embedder&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;interface&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Chunks&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Job&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Chunk&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;func&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Apply&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;idx&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Index&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;order&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;RevisionOrder&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;emb&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Embedder&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;job&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Job&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;idx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;CurrentRevision&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;job&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;DocumentID&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;job&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Revision&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Redelivery is already complete.&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;""&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;order&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Older&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;job&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Revision&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// A delayed job must not roll the index backward.&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="n"&gt;chunks&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;emb&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Chunks&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;job&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;len&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;chunks&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;errors&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;New&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"refusing to replace a document with no chunks"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;idx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ReplaceDocument&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;job&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;DocumentID&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;job&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Revision&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;chunks&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;A content hash cannot order two different revisions, so the job also needs an ordering value from the source system, such as a monotonically increasing version. Keep the hash for equality and integrity. Do not use wall-clock arrival time as truth: retries and concurrent workers can arrive out of order.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Replacement matters. Appending new chunks while leaving old ones searchable lets both revisions compete. Deleting first creates a window with no document. A staged write followed by an atomic generation switch is cleaner: build the new generation, validate it, expose it, then retire the previous one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This design has a real limitation: keeping two generations consumes extra storage, while atomic switching requires index support or an indirection layer. For a tiny wiki with rare edits, a synchronous fetch-and-replace path may be easier to operate. For a large property portfolio, the extra storage is usually a better trade-off than paying the compute cost and recovery delay of full-corpus reindexing. The threshold depends on measured update volume, chunk count, and acceptable freshness lag; no universal document count settles it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Trace the failure before replaying work
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start with one known page and a sentence whose change is unambiguous. Record its document ID, expected source revision, and a query that should retrieve the changed passage. Then walk the evidence in order.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One page. One revision. One probe.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Fetch the page and compute the normalized revision. Confirm the changed sentence survived normalization.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Find the watcher observation for that revision, including its prior revision and enqueue decision.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Find the durable job by document ID and revision. Check attempts, acknowledgements, and dead-letter state.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Find the worker commit. Compare requested revision, committed revision, chunk count, and generation.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Query retrieval with answer generation disabled. Inspect returned document IDs and revisions.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Only after retrieval is current, inspect answer caches and prompt assembly.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Stop at the first mismatch. If the source hash is new but no event exists, inspect scheduling and watcher checkpoints. If the event exists but no job does, the enqueue boundary failed. If a worker reports success but the committed revision is absent, the write acknowledgement is too weak. If direct retrieval is fresh and chat is stale, indexing is innocent; inspect cache keys and conversation state.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This sequence is deliberately narrow. Replaying one idempotent job preserves the failure signal and limits load. Rebuilding everything can saturate embedding workers, deepen the queue, and delay genuinely new policy changes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Queue depth alone is a trap. A depth of zero can mean all work completed, but it can also mean jobs were never published, were dead-lettered, or were acknowledged before commit. Track age of the oldest pending job, lag from source observation to searchable commit, and counts by terminal outcome. Alert on sustained freshness lag against an explicit objective, not one transient backlog.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;During debugging, resist changing chunk size, embedding configuration, and queue behavior together. A successful reindex with three moving inputs does not reveal which input repaired stale answers, and it makes rollback ambiguous. Hold retrieval configuration fixed, replay the one expected revision, and widen the investigation only when its boundary evidence is internally consistent. This slower-looking method usually reduces total recovery work because each observation can falsify a specific failure hypothesis.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Verify the repair and keep rollback boring
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Verification should test the contract, not merely the worker process. After replaying the affected revision, issue a retrieval-only query and require the expected document ID and revision in the result metadata. Then run the full assistant query and confirm its citation points to the current page. Test a second query for unchanged text on that page; a bad chunk replacement can fix the edited sentence while dropping neighboring material.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keep the previous index generation until those checks pass. If retrieval quality falls, switch the document pointer back and preserve both generations for analysis. Rollback should not require re-fetching a page that may have changed again.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The deployment check is small but consequential: send duplicate jobs, reverse their delivery order, interrupt a worker before commit, and interrupt it after commit but before acknowledgement. The expected result is one visible latest revision in every case. This is where idempotency earns its keep.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For operations, expose four linked measurements: source observations, queued revisions, committed revisions, and revisions returned by retrieval. Logs need the document ID and revision as structured fields. Metrics show fleet-level lag; correlated records explain one page. Avoid putting page bodies into telemetry because property documentation can contain tenant or operational details.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The decision rule is simple:&lt;/strong&gt; repair the earliest broken boundary, replay the smallest idempotent unit, and declare recovery only when retrieval returns the intended revision. This keeps index cost proportional to changed documents while giving the on-call engineer evidence stronger than "the reindex ran."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  References
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Knowledge-Intensive NLP Tasks: &lt;a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.11401" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.11401&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sybil Solutions has published &lt;strong&gt;inference-optimisation-skill&lt;/strong&gt;, an agent skill for tuning local LLM serving across decode, speculative decoding, prefill, KV-cache capacity, startup and time to first token. The project describes the procedure as hardware- and quantization-agnostic, but its immediate value for developers is narrower and practical: it replaces ad hoc flag changes with controlled experiments under a memory cap and full-context stability checks. &lt;a href="https://github.com/sybil-solutions/inference-optimisation-skill" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;The project is available on GitHub&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A benchmark loop with rejection criteria
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The prescribed loop begins by calculating theoretical ceilings, then changes one variable at a time and compares the candidate with interleaved control runs. The queue runs control, candidate and control serially; the project calls for at least six samples and medians, and accepts a candidate only when it beats both neighbouring controls without regressing another measurement by more than 3%. &lt;a href="https://github.com/sybil-solutions/inference-optimisation-skill" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;The repository documents the complete workflow&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Those controls address a measurement problem identified by the project itself: it estimates benchmark noise at ±5–10% and warns that many apparent single-run gains are drift. Before publication, a selected configuration must also pass long-context needle retrieval, a concurrent full-KV soak, acceptance gates and verification from a clean, pinned build. &lt;a href="https://github.com/sybil-solutions/inference-optimisation-skill" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;These requirements are set out in the README&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The skill can run through Claude Code or another agent that loads &lt;code&gt;SKILL.md&lt;/code&gt;-style instructions. The repository also supplies &lt;code&gt;PROMPT.md&lt;/code&gt; for direct use, along with references covering ceiling calculations, measurement traps, optimization levers, stack-specific problems and result publication. &lt;a href="https://github.com/sybil-solutions/inference-optimisation-skill" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;The repository describes both integration paths&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What the harness measures
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The bundled &lt;code&gt;bench.py&lt;/code&gt; targets OpenAI-compatible servers. It measures startup, short-prompt TTFT, single-user and concurrent decode, cold prefill at several lengths, long-context needle retrieval and peak memory with a cap watchdog. Token counts come from server usage statistics rather than streamed chunks, while failed requests, looping output and battery-powered runs are flagged; results are written to &lt;code&gt;ledger.jsonl&lt;/code&gt;. &lt;a href="https://github.com/sybil-solutions/inference-optimisation-skill" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;The benchmark behavior is documented here&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A separate &lt;code&gt;ceiling.py&lt;/code&gt; estimates limits from measured bandwidth and FLOPs plus model component sizes. &lt;code&gt;queue.sh&lt;/code&gt; serializes detachable control and candidate runs, while &lt;code&gt;mem.sh&lt;/code&gt; attempts to include driver residency on unified-memory GPUs that the README says cgroups miss. &lt;a href="https://github.com/sybil-solutions/inference-optimisation-skill" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;The repository lists these scripts and their roles&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Reported gains stay tied to their machines
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The README presents results by hardware, model, quantization and engine rather than as a universal speedup. On one Intel Arc B390 configuration running Ornith-1.5-35B-A3B with EXL3 4 bpw, SGLang XPU and MTP, it reports median single-user decode rising from 43.6 to 53 tokens per second. The same run moved 256K-token prefill from 297 to 628 tokens per second and reduced startup from 113 to 43 seconds. &lt;a href="https://github.com/sybil-solutions/inference-optimisation-skill" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;The configuration and measurements appear in the results table&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For that B390 run, the project estimates a practical single-user decode target of about 88–97 tokens per second and says the measured result reached roughly 55% of it. It reports a 256K prefill result of 628 tokens per second against an estimated ceiling of approximately 670–684. &lt;a href="https://github.com/sybil-solutions/inference-optimisation-skill" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;The README explains its interpretation of those ceilings&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Analysis:&lt;/strong&gt; The defensible interpretation is that this project contributes an acceptance standard, not a portable fastest configuration. Repeated controls, retrieval tests, soak runs and clean-build qualification make experiments slower, but they also make it harder to promote a drift-driven or short-context gain as a robust improvement. The unresolved choice remains workload priority: because decode, concurrency, prefill, TTFT, startup and KV capacity are measured separately, a team still has to decide which constraint should determine the winning configuration. &lt;a href="https://github.com/sybil-solutions/inference-optimisation-skill" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;That interpretation follows from the project’s separate metrics and qualification gates&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most grid bot backtests count one thing: how many times price crossed a grid line. Multiply by profit per grid, done. That number is almost always too optimistic. Here's what it misses, and how I model it in a simulator.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  1. Fees are paid on every fill, not every round trip
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A grid with a 0.3% step and 0.05% taker fee per side keeps only part of each step. With tighter grids the fee share grows quickly. The free calculator shows profit per grid after fees: &lt;a href="https://botrader.net/grid-bot-calculator?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=article&amp;amp;utm_campaign=kit1" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;grid bot calculator&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  2. Price leaving the range is the real risk
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Inside the range a neutral grid collects small amounts. Outside it, the bot holds the whole inventory on one side, and the open loss can be many times the collected profit. A good backtest has to report that open loss, not just "grid profit".&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  3. Futures add funding and liquidation
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Funding is paid or received on the position you hold, usually every 8 hours (some contracts settle every 4 or even 1 hour). Liquidation depends on leverage, maintenance margin and how much inventory the grid has built up, so it moves as the grid fills. I check it on every price update.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  4. Fills aren't instant at the line
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On fast moves price can jump past several lines between updates. The simulator walks the price path inside each candle and fills each crossed line in order, with slippage.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;I put all of this into &lt;a href="https://botrader.net" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Botrader&lt;/a&gt;, a grid-trading simulator that runs on live public market data with a virtual balance. It never asks for exchange keys and never places orders.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you've built your own grid backtests, I'm curious what else you model.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


  CVE-2026-123039 Recovery: Webshells, Persistence and Post-Exploitation Evidence
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Installing a fixed Check Point build addresses CVE-2026-123039. It does not remove files, accounts or trust changes created while an attacker held root. The recovery record should show whether the appliance was merely vulnerable or actually compromised, and what evidence supports that conclusion.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This distinction is central to the documented PHP2Shell campaign, where DynamicCLI escalation followed an initial foothold and preceded webshells, backdoors, scanning and destructive action.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Vulnerability overview
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Gaia OS DynamicCLI daemon runs as root and uses the world-writable Unix socket &lt;code&gt;/tmp/dynamic_socket&lt;/code&gt;. It does not authenticate the process that connects. A local user can send commands that execute as root.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;CVE-2026-123039 has a CVSS 3.1 score of 7.8 and maps to CWE-732 and CWE-862. Check Point confirmed exploitation, and CISA added it to KEV on November 13, 2026.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Mechanism and exploitation conditions
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The attacker needs a local execution context. In the investigated intrusion, PHP2Shell on Mobile Access portals supplied initial access. DynamicCLI then turned the limited portal or service position into root.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Recovery starts before cleanup. If responders delete files or restart without collecting evidence, they can lose the link between the initial access, the privilege change and the later persistence.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Impact
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The documented intrusion used root access to place PHP webshells under portal template locations. The TSGA component appeared as &lt;code&gt;/tmp/CPconfigdaemon&lt;/code&gt; and later as &lt;code&gt;slot_CPconfigdaemon&lt;/code&gt; in a boot persistence location. TSProxy was embedded in &lt;code&gt;/lib/libcpfwcommon.so&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The attackers also used a scanner, moved to a VMware ESXi host over SSH through the &lt;code&gt;vpxuser&lt;/code&gt; account and executed a wiper. Those events depend on the victim environment, but they show the scope a rooted security appliance can create.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Affected products and scope
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Review R82.20 through Take 10, R82.10 through Take 50, R82 through Take 130 and R81.20 through Take 173. Fixed takes are R82.20 Take 14, R82.10 Take 52, R82 Take 132 and R81.20 Take 174 or later.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The product scope includes Firewalls, Management and Log Servers, Multi-Domain products and Maestro Orchestrator. Migrate R81 and R81.10. Spark Firewalls and Smart-1 Cloud are not affected by this CVE.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Exposure context
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ZoomEye returned 1,077,985 Check Point product fingerprints for &lt;code&gt;app="Check Point"&lt;/code&gt; on November 23, 2026, while the CVE filter returned zero. The public count can identify visible devices, but recovery depends on internal evidence of local access and root activity.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Review local login records, DynamicCLI use, root process creation, portal template changes, unusual boot persistence, unexpected shared libraries, outbound connections and changes to policy or logging.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Remediation and mitigations
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Preserve logs and volatile state under the incident response plan. Install the fixed build or migrate the system. Check the published file hashes from the research report against the appliance and search for the named paths, while recognizing that absence of those exact indicators does not prove clean status.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Validate accounts, services, configuration and scheduled tasks against a known-good baseline. Review downstream systems reachable from the appliance, especially virtualization management and remote service accounts. Rotate credentials that were available to the compromised root environment.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Do not return the appliance to full trust until the incident owner has reconciled initial access, privilege escalation, persistence, lateral movement and containment. Retain the fixed build evidence with the recovery decision. A closed CVE and a clean baseline are separate completion checks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  References
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://research.checkpoint.com/2026/php2shell-exploitation-on-check-point-gateway/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Check Point Research: PHP2Shell exploitation on Check Point Gateway&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://support.checkpoint.com/results/sk/sk1000362" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Check Point SK1000362: CVE-2026-123039&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.cert.gov.lk/alerts/CERT-NCSOC-0233.htm" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Sri Lanka CERT: Check Point Gaia privilege escalation&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-123039" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;NVD: CVE-2026-123039&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;AI disclosure:&lt;/strong&gt; This article was written with AI assistance (Claude). The code is condensed from a scraper I maintain, and every number in the output sections came from actually running the scripts below on 2026-11-20/22.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you want to know what users think of an iOS app (yours or a competitor's), the App Store review pages are the obvious source. Scraping the HTML is painful, though: it's rendered client-side, it changes often, and it only shows a handful of reviews.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There's a much simpler route that most people don't know about. Apple still serves two plain JSON endpoints that need no API key, no login, no headless browser and, at small volumes, no proxies:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The customer-reviews RSS feed&lt;/strong&gt;, which returns the most recent (or most helpful) reviews for an app, per country storefront.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The iTunes Lookup API&lt;/strong&gt;, which returns app metadata, including the lifetime average rating and rating count.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In this tutorial we'll use both with plain Node.js &lt;code&gt;fetch&lt;/code&gt; to:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;pull recent reviews for one app from four countries,&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;turn them into a labelled JSONL dataset (&lt;code&gt;positive&lt;/code&gt; / &lt;code&gt;neutral&lt;/code&gt; / &lt;code&gt;negative&lt;/code&gt;),&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;find which words show up far more in negative reviews than in positive ones.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The example app is Duolingo (ID &lt;code&gt;570060128&lt;/code&gt;), because it has lots of reviews in many countries. Any app ID works.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The two endpoints
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The reviews feed URL looks like this:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;https://itunes.apple.com/{country}/rss/customerreviews/page={1-10}/id={appId}/sortby={mostrecent|mosthelpful}/json
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;country&lt;/code&gt; is a 2-letter storefront code (&lt;code&gt;us&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;gb&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;de&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;br&lt;/code&gt;, ...). &lt;strong&gt;Each storefront has its own reviews.&lt;/strong&gt; A US review never shows up in the German feed.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Each page has up to 50 entries, and there are at most 10 pages. That makes &lt;strong&gt;500 reviews per app per country&lt;/strong&gt; the hard ceiling.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Drop &lt;code&gt;/json&lt;/code&gt; and you get XML instead.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Lookup API is even simpler:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;https://itunes.apple.com/lookup?id=570060128&amp;amp;country=us
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;It returns &lt;code&gt;trackName&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;averageUserRating&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;userRatingCount&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;version&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;releaseNotes&lt;/code&gt;, genres, price and more. If you only have an app name, &lt;code&gt;https://itunes.apple.com/search?term=duolingo&amp;amp;entity=software&amp;amp;country=us&lt;/code&gt; gives you the ID.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To get the app ID from a store URL like &lt;code&gt;https://apps.apple.com/us/app/duolingo-language-lessons/id570060128&lt;/code&gt;, a regex is enough:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;match&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;\&lt;span class="n"&gt;d&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;6&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;match&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;/^&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;\&lt;span class="n"&gt;d&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;6&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;$&lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


  Quirks in the feed you need to handle
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The feed is old (it dates from the iTunes era) and has a few traps. I learned these the hard way:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;feed.entry&lt;/code&gt; is not always an array.&lt;/strong&gt; If a page has exactly one review, you get an object. Always normalise it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Some entries aren't reviews.&lt;/strong&gt; Depending on the response, the first entry can describe the app itself. Real reviews are the ones with an &lt;code&gt;im:rating&lt;/code&gt; field, so filter on that.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Every value is wrapped in &lt;code&gt;{ label: ... }&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/strong&gt; The rating is &lt;code&gt;e['im:rating'].label&lt;/code&gt;, a string.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;An empty or missing page means you're done.&lt;/strong&gt; Past the last page you get no &lt;code&gt;entry&lt;/code&gt; at all (or a 400/404), not an error you need to retry.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Rate limits are real but generous.&lt;/strong&gt; At a few requests per second you're fine. If you go faster you'll see &lt;code&gt;429&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;5xx&lt;/code&gt; responses, so back off and retry.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 1: fetch reviews and build the dataset
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is the whole script. It has no dependencies and needs Node 18+ for the global &lt;code&gt;fetch&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// reviews.mjs - App Store reviews for one app across countries -&amp;gt; sentiment JSONL&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;writeFileSync&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'node:fs'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;APP_ID&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;argv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;??&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'570060128'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;           &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Duolingo&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;COUNTRIES&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;argv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;??&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'us,gb,de,br'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;split&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;','&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sleep&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ms&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;setTimeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ms&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;getJson&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;++&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;fetch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
