&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;nNeg&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;rows&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;filter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'negative'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;nPos&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;rows&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;filter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'positive'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;scored&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;neg&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;filter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;n&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;4&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pNeg&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;n&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;nNeg&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pPos&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;pos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;??&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;nPos&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;inNeg&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;inPos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;??&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ratio&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;pNeg&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pPos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toFixed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;sort&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ratio&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ratio&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;slice&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sb"&gt;`negative reviews: ${nNeg}, positive: ${nPos}`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;of&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;scored&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sb"&gt;`${s.w.padEnd(14)} neg ${s.inNeg}  pos ${s.inPos}  x${s.ratio}`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


  Real output
&lt;/h3&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;$ node themes.mjs
negative reviews: 46, positive: 144
paid           neg 5  pos 0  x18.3
enough         neg 5  pos 0  x18.3
spend          neg 5  pos 0  x18.3
duplicates     neg 4  pos 0  x15.2
luck           neg 4  pos 0  x15.2
subscription   neg 4  pos 0  x15.2
gems           neg 5  pos 1  x9.1
had            neg 9  pos 3  x7.6
collect        neg 4  pos 1  x7.6
either         neg 4  pos 1  x7.6
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Reading the reviews behind &lt;code&gt;duplicates&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;luck&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;gems&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;collect&lt;/code&gt; tells a clear story: a limited-time event where users collect puzzle pieces, keep getting duplicates, and feel pushed to buy gems with real money to finish the set. &lt;code&gt;subscription&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;paid&lt;/code&gt; point at a second, older complaint about trials and billing. That's two concrete product issues, found from 200 English reviews in under a minute of compute.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;With a sample this small, treat the list as "where to start reading", not as statistics. Words like &lt;code&gt;had&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;either&lt;/code&gt; are noise that a bigger sample or bigrams would wash out.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Where to take the dataset next
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Better labels.&lt;/strong&gt; Run a multilingual sentiment model (for example, an XLM-R based model from Hugging Face) on &lt;code&gt;title + text&lt;/code&gt; and compare it with the star label. The reviews where they disagree ("5 stars, but the new update is awful") are often the most interesting ones.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Version tracking.&lt;/strong&gt; Every row has &lt;code&gt;version&lt;/code&gt;. Group the negative share by version and you get a cheap regression detector for releases.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Competitor comparison.&lt;/strong&gt; Run the same script for 3-5 apps in a category and compare complaint themes side by side.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Most helpful vs most recent.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;sortby=mosthelpful&lt;/code&gt; gives you the reviews other users upvoted, which skews towards long, detailed reviews. It's a different sample, so don't mix the two without a column that says which one it came from.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The limits (read this before you build on it)
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;500 reviews per app per country, recent only.&lt;/strong&gt; The feed is a window, not an archive. For a popular app, 500 reviews might cover only a couple of days. If you need history, run it on a schedule and store everything with &lt;code&gt;id&lt;/code&gt; as the dedupe key.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Star-only ratings are invisible.&lt;/strong&gt; You only get reviews with text. The rating distribution in the feed is not the app's real rating distribution, as the 4.72 vs 3.84 gap shows.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;No developer replies.&lt;/strong&gt; The feed doesn't include the developer's response to a review.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Country is not language.&lt;/strong&gt; The US feed in my run contained a review in Russian. Filter by detected language if your NLP step assumes English.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;It's a legacy endpoint.&lt;/strong&gt; Apple doesn't promote this feed and could change or remove it. Build your pipeline so a missing &lt;code&gt;feed.entry&lt;/code&gt; fails loudly instead of silently writing zero rows.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Be polite.&lt;/strong&gt; Apple's Search/Lookup docs suggest staying around 20 calls per minute. The reviews feed tolerated more in my tests, but pace your requests and back off on &lt;code&gt;429&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Personal data.&lt;/strong&gt; The feed includes reviewer nicknames under &lt;code&gt;author.name&lt;/code&gt;. I leave them out by default. If you keep them, you're storing personal data, so think about GDPR and your use case.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;iOS only.&lt;/strong&gt; Google Play reviews need a completely different approach.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  If you'd rather not maintain this yourself
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Running the script once is easy. Running it every day across 20 countries and 50 apps, with retries, dedupe, scheduling and exports, is where it turns into a small maintenance job.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I packaged the same approach as an Apify actor. &lt;strong&gt;Full disclosure: it's ours, and it's paid per use&lt;/strong&gt; through Apify's pay-per-event pricing: it's free to use until December 6, 2026, and from then it costs about $0.40 per 1,000 reviews plus $0.002 per app details record. It adds app search by keyword, star filters, up to 500 reviews per country, an app-details record from the Lookup API, scheduling and CSV/Excel/JSON export. Reviewer names are off unless you turn them on. In a local test run it pulled the same 400 Duolingo reviews from four countries with zero failed requests.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you also want to feed an app's website, docs or changelog into the same LLM pipeline next to the reviews, our &lt;a href="https://apify.com/agebreak/website-to-markdown" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Website to Markdown&lt;/a&gt; actor converts pages into clean Markdown. It's built the same way: plain HTTP and no browser.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The review scraper is here: &lt;a href="https://apify.com/agebreak/apple-app-store-reviews" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Apple App Store Reviews Scraper (Multi-Country, No Proxy)&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But honestly, the two scripts above are about 100 lines. If you only need a one-off dataset, copy them and go.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Forecasting usually means exporting data, training a model and writing the results back, so many teams skip it. The &lt;code&gt;ai_forecast&lt;/code&gt; function in Databricks SQL does the forecast in one query, and version 2 adds holiday support, covariates and non-negative forecasts on top of a time series foundation model. This guide builds a daily sales forecast step by step: you prepare the input, run a basic forecast, switch to version 2, add holidays, add a covariate and constrain the output to non-negative values. In testing, the biggest gain did not come from the new model, but from telling it about planned promotions. The complete code for this guide is on GitHub: &lt;a href="https://github.com/ven-nbondala-tf/databricks-release-lab/tree/main/databricks/ai-forecast" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ai-forecast&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;  &lt;iframe src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/-7MijS-_BKQ" width="710" height="399"&gt;
  &lt;/iframe&gt;
&lt;br&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Forecast error for five setups, from version 1 to planned promotions, in 30 seconds.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


  What is ai_forecast in Databricks SQL
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;ai_forecast&lt;/code&gt; is a table-valued function that reads a table of history and returns forecasted values with an upper and a lower bound. You call it in the FROM clause, so the result is an ordinary table you can filter, join or save.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Version 2 is generally available in Databricks SQL, announced on November 18, 2026. Version 1 is in Public Preview.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Version 2 uses a time series foundation model. Version 1 uses a prophet-like piecewise linear and seasonality model.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It forecasts one or more value columns, optionally per group, and supports holidays, covariates and non-negative forecasts in version 2.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It runs on the compute you submit it from, with no separate inference cost.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The diagram shows what goes in and what comes out.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F6y71tfkfty1ebg15rvth.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F6y71tfkfty1ebg15rvth.png" alt="A flow diagram. History rows, optional covariate rows and optional arguments feed ai_forecast with version 2. It returns forecast rows only, with a forecast, an upper and a lower column for each value column." width="799" height="364"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;em&gt;The inputs and outputs of ai_forecast version 2.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


  When to use ai_forecast
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use &lt;code&gt;ai_forecast&lt;/code&gt; when your data is already in a table, you want a forecast from SQL, and a general-purpose model is accurate enough: sales, traffic, demand or revenue per day or another regular interval, for one series or many groups. It fits well inside a scheduled query, a dashboard dataset or a pipeline step.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use a custom model when you need control over the model itself, such as custom features, your own training and evaluation loop, or a model registered and served separately. Forecasting in Databricks SQL with &lt;code&gt;ai_forecast&lt;/code&gt; is a fast starting point, and a custom model is the next step when its accuracy is not enough.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


  Prerequisites
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A Pro or Serverless SQL warehouse. AI Functions are not available on Classic SQL warehouses.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Read access to the table with your history, and permission to create tables if you want to save the forecast.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Version 2 ran on a serverless SQL warehouse with no preview setting turned on in the test workspace. The function page says that for some workspaces, including those with the compliance security profile, a workspace admin must turn on "AI Forecast V2" on the Previews page. If version 2 is not available, ask your admin to check that setting.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 1: Prepare the input
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The function needs a time column of type DATE or TIMESTAMP, and a value column that can be cast to DOUBLE. Create a table with one row per day and region. The setup script in the repository builds two years of daily sales for two regions, a promo flag and a second series that trends close to zero.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight sql"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;CREATE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;SCHEMA&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;IF&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;NOT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;EXISTS&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;catalog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;forecast_demo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;CREATE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;OR&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;REPLACE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;TABLE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;catalog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;forecast_demo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sales_daily&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;ds&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;DATE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;region&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;STRING&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;sales&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;DOUBLE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;promo&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;BOOLEAN&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The examples use the data before 2026-11-16 as history, and compare the forecast with the 60 days that follow. The generated data runs to mid-January 2027, so the last 60 days can act as "actuals" to compare against. With real data, you hold out the most recent 60 days instead, and for the final forecast you pass all the history you have.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 2: Run a basic forecast
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A forecast needs the table, the end of the horizon, and the names of the time and value columns. Add &lt;code&gt;group_col&lt;/code&gt; to forecast each region independently.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight sql"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;SELECT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;FROM&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;AI_FORECAST&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;TABLE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;SELECT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;region&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sales&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;FROM&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;catalog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;forecast_demo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sales_daily&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;WHERE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ds&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;DATE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'2026-11-16'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;horizon&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'2027-01-15'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;time_col&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'ds'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;value_col&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'sales'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;group_col&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'region'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The result has the columns &lt;code&gt;ds&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;region&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;sales_forecast&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;sales_upper&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;sales_lower&lt;/code&gt;. The horizon is right-exclusive, so this query returns the 60 days from 2026-11-16 to 2027-01-14. The output contains forecasted rows only, not the history. This query uses version 1, because version 1 is the default when you do not pass &lt;code&gt;version&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 3: Switch to version 2
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Version 2 is recommended for most use cases, and you select it with the &lt;code&gt;version&lt;/code&gt; argument. Add it to the same query.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight sql"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;SELECT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;FROM&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;AI_FORECAST&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;TABLE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;SELECT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;region&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sales&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;FROM&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;catalog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;forecast_demo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sales_daily&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;WHERE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ds&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;DATE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'2026-11-16'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;horizon&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'2027-01-15'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;time_col&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'ds'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;value_col&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'sales'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;group_col&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'region'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;version&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'2'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Version 1 is on a deprecation path, and the default will change to version 2 in an upcoming release. Pass &lt;code&gt;version&lt;/code&gt; explicitly so your results do not change when it does.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 4: Add holidays
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Holiday effects are modeled when you pass a region code, such as &lt;code&gt;'US'&lt;/code&gt;. Use it when your series dips or peaks around public holidays.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight sql"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;SELECT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;FROM&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;AI_FORECAST&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;TABLE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;SELECT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;region&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sales&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;FROM&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;catalog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;forecast_demo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sales_daily&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;WHERE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ds&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;DATE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'2026-11-16'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;horizon&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'2027-01-15'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;time_col&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'ds'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;value_col&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'sales'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;group_col&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'region'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;holiday_region&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'US'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;version&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'2'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The chart compares the forecasts with the actual values for the East region. Sales dip on Thanksgiving, Christmas and New Year in the test data. The forecast with holidays predicts part of each dip, and the forecasts without holidays predict none.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fiplf8af1g5g0x25n4t46.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fiplf8af1g5g0x25n4t46.png" alt="A line chart. The actual sales line dips sharply on Thanksgiving, Christmas and New Year. The version 1 and version 2 forecasts stay flat through those days. The version 2 forecast with US holidays dips on all three days, by less than the actual values. A shaded band shows the version 2 prediction interval." width="800" height="446"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Actual sales and forecasts for the 60 held-out days in the East region.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 5: Add a covariate
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A covariate is a column that explains the value, such as a promotion flag. To use one as a future covariate, add rows that cover the forecast horizon with the covariate filled in and the value column set to NULL.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight sql"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;SELECT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;FROM&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;AI_FORECAST&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;TABLE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;SELECT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;region&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sales&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;promo&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;FROM&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;catalog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;forecast_demo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sales_daily&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;WHERE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ds&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;DATE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'2026-11-16'&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;UNION&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;ALL&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;SELECT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;region&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;CAST&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;NULL&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;AS&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;DOUBLE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;AS&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sales&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;promo&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;FROM&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;catalog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;forecast_demo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sales_daily&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;WHERE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ds&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;DATE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'2026-11-16'&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;horizon&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'2027-01-15'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;time_col&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'ds'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;value_col&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'sales'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;group_col&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'region'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;covariate_col&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'promo'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;version&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'2'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;If you fill the covariate only on the history rows, &lt;code&gt;ai_forecast&lt;/code&gt; treats it as a past-only covariate. That is not an error, but the forecast gains little, because the model cannot see the promotions that are planned.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 6: Constrain the forecast to non-negative values
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Set &lt;code&gt;positive_only&lt;/code&gt; for series that cannot go below zero, such as returns, units or counts. Without it, the lower bound of the interval can fall below zero even when the forecast itself does not.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight sql"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;SELECT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;FROM&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;AI_FORECAST&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;TABLE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;SELECT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;returns&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;FROM&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;catalog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;forecast_demo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;returns_daily&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;WHERE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ds&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;DATE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'2026-11-16'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;horizon&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'2027-01-15'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;time_col&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'ds'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;value_col&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'returns'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;positive_only&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;TRUE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;version&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'2'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The chart shows a daily returns series that trends close to zero. In the test, none of the point forecasts were negative either way. Without &lt;code&gt;positive_only&lt;/code&gt;, all 60 lower bounds were below zero, and with it the lower bounds stop at zero.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Ftihrbuxs7t97gdxfg2uc.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Ftihrbuxs7t97gdxfg2uc.png" alt="Two line charts of daily returns with a forecast line and a shaded interval. In the left chart the lower edge of the interval goes below zero. In the right chart the lower edge stops at zero." width="799" height="411"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;em&gt;The same near-zero series forecast without and with positive_only.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


  Example: forecast sales by region with planned promotions
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A retailer plans promotions for the next 60 days and wants the sales forecast per region to reflect them. The input is the history plus the planned horizon rows with &lt;code&gt;promo&lt;/code&gt; set and &lt;code&gt;sales&lt;/code&gt; NULL, which is the query in Step 5. You save the result so a dashboard can read it.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight sql"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;CREATE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;OR&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;REPLACE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;TABLE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;catalog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;forecast_demo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;fc_promo&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;AS&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;SELECT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;FROM&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;AI_FORECAST&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;TABLE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;SELECT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;region&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sales&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;promo&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;FROM&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;catalog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;forecast_demo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sales_daily&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;WHERE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ds&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;DATE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'2026-11-16'&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;UNION&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;ALL&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;SELECT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;region&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;CAST&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;NULL&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;AS&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;DOUBLE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;promo&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;FROM&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;catalog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;forecast_demo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sales_daily&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;WHERE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ds&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;DATE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'2026-11-16'&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;horizon&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'2027-01-15'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;time_col&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'ds'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;value_col&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'sales'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;group_col&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'region'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;covariate_col&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'promo'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;version&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'2'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;To measure accuracy, join the forecast to the actual values for the same days and average the absolute difference.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight sql"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;SELECT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;region&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
       &lt;span class="nb"&gt;ROUND&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;AVG&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;ABS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sales_forecast&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sales&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;AS&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;mae&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;FROM&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;catalog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;forecast_demo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;fc_promo&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;JOIN&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;catalog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;forecast_demo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sales_daily&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;ON&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ds&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ds&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;AND&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;region&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;region&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;GROUP&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;BY&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;region&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The query returns the mean absolute error (MAE) for each region. Running it on each forecast gives the table below for the 60 held-out days. The main finding is the covariate result: a future covariate roughly halved the error, while a past-only covariate gave no real gain. The model can only use what it knows about the forecast window, so planned promotions help and promotions it never sees ahead of time do not. Version 2 with defaults is modestly better than version 1, and holidays help on the holiday days.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fjjb9ay42ga2p8n9ef6gm.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fjjb9ay42ga2p8n9ef6gm.png" alt="A table with the East and West mean absolute error for five forecasts. Version 1 with defaults is 40.38 and 33.17. Version 2 with defaults is 36.94 and 32.53. Version 2 with US holidays is 33.77 and 31.29. Version 2 with promo as a future covariate is 21.74 and 16.68. Version 2 with promo as a past-only covariate is 37.67 and 32.75." width="800" height="361"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Mean absolute error for each forecast on the 60 held-out days, lower is better.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Forecast&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;East MAE&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;West MAE&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Version 1, defaults&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;40.38&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;33.17&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Version 2, defaults&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;36.94&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;32.53&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Version 2 with holiday_region US&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;33.77&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;31.29&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Version 2 with promo as a future covariate&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;21.74&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;16.68&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Version 2 with promo as a past-only covariate&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;37.67&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;32.75&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The data in this guide is generated. It has a built-in promo effect, with promotion days selling 30% more, and built-in dips on US holidays, so these results show how the arguments behave, not the accuracy you will see on real data. Use them to compare the options, and run the same comparison on your own history. The full example, including setup and cleanup, is in the &lt;a href="https://github.com/ven-nbondala-tf/databricks-release-lab/tree/main/databricks/ai-forecast" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ai-forecast&lt;/a&gt; folder of the repository.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Things to know
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Version 1 is the default. Pass &lt;code&gt;version =&amp;gt; '2'&lt;/code&gt; to use version 2, and do it even after the default changes so your queries state what they use.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The version 2 arguments &lt;code&gt;covariate_col&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;holiday_region&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;positive_only&lt;/code&gt; work only with version 2. If you leave out &lt;code&gt;version&lt;/code&gt;, the query fails with &lt;code&gt;PYTHON_TVF_UNSUPPORTED_ARGS_FOR_VERSION&lt;/code&gt;, for example: "The function &lt;code&gt;AI_FORECAST&lt;/code&gt; was called with &lt;code&gt;version =&amp;gt; '1'&lt;/code&gt;, which does not support the following argument(s): holiday_region." Add &lt;code&gt;version =&amp;gt; '2'&lt;/code&gt; to fix it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The horizon is right-exclusive, and the output holds forecast rows only. To plot history and forecast together, UNION the history with the result.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A future covariate needs rows for the horizon with the covariate filled in and the value column NULL. Without them the covariate is past-only and adds little.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The frequency is the mode of the most recent observations in each group. If you set &lt;code&gt;frequency&lt;/code&gt;, it must match the granularity of your data.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Best practices
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Hold out the last part of your history, forecast it and compare, as the tables in this guide do, before you trust a forecast.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Pass &lt;code&gt;holiday_region&lt;/code&gt; when your series follows public holidays, and &lt;code&gt;positive_only&lt;/code&gt; when it cannot go below zero.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Add &lt;code&gt;group_col&lt;/code&gt; instead of writing one call per group. A grouped call returns the same values as separate calls.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Save the forecast in a table and schedule the query, so &lt;a href="https://medium.com/@nagarajubru/databricks-dashboards-permissions-refresh-553e82f3e4a1" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;dashboards&lt;/a&gt; read a table instead of calling the function.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Summary
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;ai_forecast&lt;/code&gt; returns a forecast with an upper and a lower bound from a single SQL query, and version 2 adds holidays, covariates and non-negative forecasts. The two things to remember: pass &lt;code&gt;version =&amp;gt; '2'&lt;/code&gt; to use the new options, and give it your planned covariate values, since that is where the biggest gain came from. Use it when your history is in a table and you want a forecast from SQL without training a model.&lt;/p&gt;

